# Home Assistant Configuration

This is a Home Assistant configuration directory.

## Environment

- **Working directory**: `/homeassistant` — the same directory HA itself sees as `/config`. Use `/config/...` inside anything HA executes (`shell_command`, `python_script`), and `/homeassistant/...` for your own tool calls.
- **URL**: http://homeassistant.local:8123
- Do not hardcode version numbers anywhere. Read them when needed: `ha core info` (Core version, update availability), `ha os info` (HAOS version).

## Directory Structure

- `configuration.yaml` - Main configuration file
- `automations.yaml` - Automation definitions
- `scripts.yaml` - Script definitions
- `scenes.yaml` - Scene definitions
- `secrets.yaml` - Secret values (API keys, passwords, webhook ids). Never commit or share it; work with it through `ha-secrets`
- `customize.yaml` - Entity customizations
- `.storage/` - Home Assistant's internal state. Never edit it, and never read or decode it to answer a question: entities, devices, areas, helpers, integrations, dashboards and traces all have an `ha-*` tool or a template function (table below). If none fits, say so rather than parsing the JSON.

## CLI Tools

The `ha-*` tools are on `PATH`. Call them by bare name; a path form such as `./ha-entities` is rewritten to the bare name before it runs, so it is never what you meant. If a call errors, fix the call instead of falling back to ad-hoc WebSocket scripts via `ha_lib` or `curl`. All of them read a long-lived token from `HA_TOKEN` or `/homeassistant/.claude/ha_token` and print setup steps when it is missing; `ha-integrations`, `ha-entities update`/`rename`, `ha-areas create`/`update`/`delete` and the Supervisor half of `ha-system repairs` need an administrator's token.

Editing a `.yaml` file under `/homeassistant` triggers an automatic parse check (syntax plus duplicate top-level keys). If it fails, the error comes back to you as a tool error; fix it before reloading.

Hooks guard a few operations, whatever the permission mode:

- **Blocked outright**, so retrying will not help: recursive deletes of a mapped directory, `mkfs`/`dd` onto a device, piping a download into a shell, and shell writes into `.storage`.
- **Ask the user first**: any other shell command touching `.storage` beyond a plain read, any shell command naming `secrets.yaml`, `ha-secrets get` and `add`, `ha-entities rename`, `ha-integrations delete` and `disable`, `ha-areas delete` (a `--dry-run` of these four does not ask), any `ha-service` call with `--unsafe`, `ha-dashboard delete`, and every `ha` CLI command except reads (`info`, `logs`, `stats`, `check`, bare listings such as `ha apps`) and a few routine ones (`ha apps start`, `ha backups new`, `ha refresh-updates`).

A blocked command means the job has a tool (`ha-dashboard`, `ha-secrets`, ...) or belongs in the Home Assistant UI; do not work around the guard.

## Discover, Never Guess

Entities, areas, services, and integrations change without this file changing. **Never write an entity ID, service field, area name, or integration name from memory or from this file; look it up first.** The skills are the source of truth:

| Question | Use |
|---|---|
| What is X right now? (state, battery, temperature, open/closed, unavailable) What is the entity ID for X? | `FindEntities` (`ha-entities get` / `list --search`) |
| What domains / scripts / automations exist? Which lights (or other entities) are in an area or carry a label? | `FindEntities` (`domains`, `scripts`, `automations`, `list --area` / `--label`) |
| Which areas, floors or labels exist? Everything an area or floor holds, or what carries a label? | `ManageAreas` (`ha-areas list` / `show`) |
| Which device owns an entity, which entities come from an integration? | `TestTemplate` (`device_attr`, `integration_entities`, ...) |
| Turn something on or off, run a script, activate a scene, set a thermostat, send a notification | `CallService` (`ha-service show`, then `call`) |
| Pausing or switching off an automation | `CallService` (`automation.turn_off`), not a registry disable |
| What fields does this service take? What are the valid values? | `CallService`: check `ha-service show` before writing service data anywhere |
| Setting helper/virtual state (booleans, selects, numbers, timers, counters) | `SetState` (`ha-state`); raw service calls on these domains are refused |
| Creating a helper | Toggles, dropdowns, numbers, text, datetime: an `input_*` block in YAML plus `ReloadConfig`. Template, group, threshold, utility meter: `ManageIntegration` (`handlers --helpers`) |
| Creating, renaming, changing (floor, icon, colour, aliases) or deleting an area, floor or label | `ManageAreas` (`ha-areas`) |
| Does this Jinja template work? What does it evaluate to? | `TestTemplate` |
| What happened in the past? How long / how often / when last? | `QueryHistory` |
| Who or what changed X? Was it an automation, a person, the device itself? | `QueryHistory` (`ha-history logbook`) |
| Something is unavailable or stopped working | `FindEntities get`, then `ManageIntegration` (`list --failing`), then `QueryHistory` for since when |
| Why didn't this automation fire, or what did it actually do? | `DebugAutomation` (traces, not YAML guesswork) |
| Renaming an entity or device, moving it to an area, adding or removing a label, icon, hide/disable, changing an entity_id | `UpdateEntity` (`ha-entities update` / `rename`) |
| What uses this entity or device? Is it safe to rename, remove or replace? | `FindReferences` (`ha-entities refs`); covers UI dashboards, helpers and device triggers that a grep misses |
| Adding, configuring, renaming, reloading, disabling or removing an integration; which integrations are failing | `ManageIntegration` (`ha-integrations`); **never** read or edit `.storage/core.config_entries` |
| Which secrets exist; creating, referencing or test-firing a webhook; adding an API key | `ManageSecrets` (`ha-secrets`); **never** cat, grep, Read or append to `secrets.yaml`, and never curl a webhook by hand |
| Anything touching dashboards, views, cards, badges | `EditDashboard`; **never** edit `.storage/lovelace.*` by hand |
| Restarting, stopping or updating an app (add-on), its logs, an app that won't start | `ManageSystem` (`ha apps`) |
| What updates are waiting? Update X; what changed in it? | `ManageSystem` (`ha-system updates`) |
| What are these repairs? Is the system healthy, supported, low on disk? | `ManageSystem` (`ha-system repairs`) |
| Applying YAML changes | `ReloadConfig` (`ha-reload`); prefers hot reload over restart |

## Supervisor Commands

- `ha core check` validates `configuration.yaml`; `yamllint <file>` for any other YAML
- `ha core restart` only for new YAML-configured integrations or logger/recorder/http changes; everything else is `ha-reload`
- Logs: `ha core logs -n 200`, `ha apps logs <slug> -n 200`, `ha supervisor logs -n 200`. Always pass `-n`; `-f` streams forever and the command never returns
- Add-ons are apps in the `ha` CLI (`ha apps`; `ha addons` is an old alias), addressed by slug, not by name; `ManageSystem` covers them
- `ha backups new --name <name>` before anything large
- Custom components: `ls custom_components/` and read each `manifest.json` (repo, version, `iot_class`); never trust a list written down anywhere

## Gotchas

- Older UI-created scripts may have numeric IDs; map them to aliases with `ha-entities scripts`, and prefer descriptive slugs for anything new
- Some `light.*`/`switch.*` entities are UI group helpers aggregating others; check with `FindEntities` before assuming an entity maps to one physical device
- Multiple radio stacks can coexist (ZHA, Zigbee2MQTT, Thread/Matter); the owning integration determines a device's trigger style, so confirm which stack owns a device before writing triggers for it
- Devices that are unplugged part of the year report `unavailable` for months; check history before treating that as a fault
